  "hypothesis": "Question: For which domains do the effect-size estimates tend to be larger in original studies compared to replication studies?  Method (per approved plan): within each domain, compute the signed paired difference d = fiso - fisr per row (positive => original larger); test Ha: median(d) > 0 with a one-sided paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test; report one-sided paired t-test sensitivity, bootstrap BCa 95% CI on the median, t-based 95% CI on the mean; adjust across the 3 discipline tests with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR (Holm also noted). relative_es is descriptive only; sign-flipped Economics rows are retained.\n\nPer-discipline results (primary):\n- Cognitive (n=34; sign-flips=5): median d = 0.249 [BCa 95% CI 0.207, 0.348]; mean d = 0.289 (SD 0.359) [t 95% CI 0.164, 0.415]; Wilcoxon one-sided p = 3.233e-06; p_BH = 4.85e-06; p_Holm = 6.467e-06; t one-sided p = 2.265e-05; mean(relative_es) = 0.509 (descriptive).\n- Social (n=34; sign-flips=10): median d = 0.232 [BCa 95% CI 0.166, 0.373]; mean d = 0.233 (SD 0.203) [t 95% CI 0.162, 0.304]; Wilcoxon one-sided p = 5.771e-07; p_BH = 1.731e-06; p_Holm = 1.731e-06; t one-sided p = 6.525e-08; mean(relative_es) = 0.278 (descriptive).\n- Economics (n=18; sign-flips=3): median d = 0.143 [BCa 95% CI 0.056, 0.293]; mean d = 0.266 (SD 0.365) [t 95% CI 0.084, 0.447]; Wilcoxon one-sided p = 0.0004196; p_BH = 0.0004196; p_Holm = 0.0004196; t one-sided p = 0.003327; mean(relative_es) = 0.657 (descriptive).\n\nProject-level sensitivity (project.x):\n- Psychology (n=68; sign-flips=15): median d = 0.242 [BCa 95% CI 0.207, 0.314]; mean d = 0.261 (SD 0.291); Wilcoxon p = 9.48e-10; p_BH = 1.896e-09; t p = 1.466e-10; mean(relative_es) = 0.394.\n- Experimental Economics (n=18; sign-flips=3): median d = 0.143 [BCa 95% CI 0.056, 0.293]; mean d = 0.266 (SD 0.365); Wilcoxon p = 0.0004196; p_BH = 0.0004196; t p = 0.003327; mean(relative_es) = 0.657.\n\nCompact conclusion (grounded in BH-adjusted p-values across 3 discipline tests):\nDomains where original-study effect-size estimates are SIGNIFICANTLY larger than replication estimates (one-sided Wilcoxon, Benjamini-Hochberg-adjusted across disciplines, alpha=0.05): Social (p_BH=1.731e-06, n=34), Cognitive (p_BH=4.85e-06, n=34), Economics (p_BH=0.0004196, n=18). Domains without a statistically significant one-sided difference after BH correction: none.\n\nNatural-language hypothesis: Across every studied domain - Cognitive, Social, and Economics - effect-size estimates are systematically larger in original studies than in their replications. The asymmetry is most pronounced in Cognitive Psychology (median fiso-fisr = 0.25, adjusted p = 4.85e-06) and Social Psychology (median 0.23, adjusted p = 1.73e-06), and is still present and decisive in Economics (median 0.14, adjusted p = 4.20e-04). The direction is uniformly positive: original > replication in all three domains at the Fisher-z scale."
